task: dataflow-epochs
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-epochs
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfe
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 6 (first half)

why: everything so far is a BATCH engine that happens to run across
  machines. A partition is read to its end, its partials are merged
  once, and the answer is produced once. A stream has no end, so
  every one of those three sentences has to change.

what, and the ordering is forced rather than chosen:

  1. `Sink`'s coordinator side becomes a FOLD. It has `result(ws): R`
     today, which can only be called when there is nothing left to
     come. It gains a running state `S` with `absorb(s, ws,
     watermark)` and `emit(s)`, and `result` becomes
     `emit(absorb(empty, ws, MaxValue))` — so no existing behaviour
     moves, and the batch answer stays the definition of correct.

     This is the load-bearing change: without it the coordinator can
     never retire a pane, and "streaming" would mean holding every
     boundary pane for ever.

  2. THE WATERMARK IS THE MINIMUM OVER THE PARTITIONS, not the
     maximum. In a batch run the coordinator knows every partition's
     greatest event time before anything folds; in a stream it knows
     only what each has reported SO FAR, and a partition that has
     not advanced holds the whole stream back. That is the standard
     rule and it is also the only correct one.

  3. An EPOCH: workers keep their operator state between rounds
     (`Open` / `Advance` / `Close`), each round advances every
     partition by a bounded amount, and the coordinator retires what
     the global watermark closed.

acceptance: the same job streamed in K epochs answers what the batch
  run answers — the same panes, the same drops. That is a strong bar
  because it can only hold if the windows survive an epoch boundary
  and the watermark is right at every step.

NOT in this lane, and named so it is not assumed: checkpointing and
  recovery from an offset (the worker's state is in memory and dies
  with it), and exactly-once at the sink. Those are 6b and 6c, and
  okay-persist already has the log and the offsets they need.

predict: the refactor in (1) will be mechanical and the watermark in
  (2) will be where a test fails first — because "min over
  partitions" and "max so far" agree on a feed where every partition
  advances evenly, which is exactly what a first test would use.
